{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-complement", "rel": "requires", "dst": "apostol:element-orthogonal-to-set", "origin": "source", "evidence": {"file": "15.14.md", "quote": "The set of all elements orthogonal to S is denoted by $S^{\\perp}$"}}
{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-complement-notation", "rel": "alias-of", "dst": "apostol:orthogonal-complement", "origin": "source", "evidence": {"file": "15.14.md", "quote": "In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S."}}
{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-complement-is-subspace", "rel": "requires", "dst": "apostol:orthogonal-complement-notation", "origin": "source", "evidence": {"file": "15.14.md", "quote": "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one."}}
{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-decomposition-theorem", "rel": "requires", "dst": "apostol:orthogonal-complement", "origin": "source", "evidence": {"file": "15.14.md", "quote": "Then every element x in V can be represented uniquely as a sum of two elements, one in S and one in $S^{\\perp}$"}}
{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-decomposition-theorem", "rel": "implies", "dst": "apostol:orthogonal-decomposition", "origin": "source", "evidence": {"file": "15.14.md", "quote": "x = s + s ^ {\\perp}, \\quad \\text { where } \\quad s \\in S \\quad \\text { and } \\quad s ^ {\\perp} \\in S ^ {\\perp}."}}
{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-decomposition-theorem", "rel": "implies", "dst": "apostol:pythagorean-formula", "origin": "source", "evidence": {"file": "15.14.md", "quote": "Moreover, the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula"}}
{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-decomposition", "rel": "requires", "dst": "apostol:orthogonal-complement", "origin": "model"}
{"src": "apostol:orthogonal-decomposition", "rel": "requires", "dst": "apostol:element-orthogonal-to-set", "origin": "source", "evidence": {"file": "15.14.md", "quote": "Therefore $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to every element in $S$ , which means that $s^\\perp \\in S^\\perp$"}}
